 "answer": "The air quality in Kuala Lumpur is ranging from **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy**, depending on the specific monitoring station and index used. \n\nThe latest real-time readings show:\n\n* **Real-Time AQI (AQICN / Malaysia DOE):** **173 (Unhealthy)**\n  * **Primary Pollutant:** Fine particulate matter ($PM_{2.5}$) at an AQI of 173\n  * **$PM_{10}$ AQI:** 77 (Moderate)\n  * **Other Pollutants ($O_3$, $NO_2$, $SO_2$, $CO$):** Good / Low (AQI 0–16)\n* **IQAir Index:** Reports localized readings reaching up to **238 (Very Unhealthy)** in parts of Kuala Lumpur, with neighboring areas in Selangor (such as Klang and Petaling Jaya) recording AQI levels between **180 and 186 (Unhealthy)**.\n\n**Health Recommendations:**\n* **Sensitive Groups** (children, the elderly, and individuals with respiratory or heart conditions) should avoid prolonged or heavy outdoor exertion. \n* **General Public:** Everyone may begin to experience adverse health effects at these levels. It is advisable to limit strenuous outdoor activities, keep windows closed to prevent outdoor haze/pollution from entering indoors, use an air purifier if available, and wear a well-fitted mask (such as an N95/KN95) if you need to be outside for extended periods.",
